# Empirical Evaluation Plan: Universal Feature Extractor for Atari Games

## 1. Project Overview & Objectives

The goal of this project is to evaluate whether a single, universally pre-trained discrete representation model (**Finite Scalar Quantization VAE**) can serve as a drop-in feature extractor across diverse Atari 2600 environments.

This evaluation explicitly investigates:

* **Downstream Performance:** Can frozen visual representations match or exceed the performance of representations learned from scratch?
* **Training Efficiency:** Does freezing the convolutional backbone reduce overall wall-clock training time or sample complexity?
* **Generalization:** Can a representation trained on a specific subset of games successfully generalize to entirely unseen environments?

---

## 2. Experimental Design & Game Splits

To rigorously test interpolation (optimization) versus extrapolation (generalization), the 15 selected games are decoupled into two distinct operational sets.

### Training Set (Representation Learning)

The FSQ-VAE backbone was trained for **125 epochs** exclusively on expert trajectories ($50,000$ frames per game) harvested from the following 11 environments:

* `BeamRiderNoFrameskip-v4`
* `BreakoutNoFrameskip-v4`
* `DemonAttackNoFrameskip-v4`
* `EnduroNoFrameskip-v4`
* `MsPacmanNoFrameskip-v4`
* `PongNoFrameskip-v4`
* `QbertNoFrameskip-v4`
* `RiverraidNoFrameskip-v4`
* `RoadRunnerNoFrameskip-v4`
* `SeaquestNoFrameskip-v4`
* `SpaceInvadersNoFrameskip-v4`

### Evaluation Set (Zero-Shot Generalization Test)

To evaluate out-of-distribution visual generalization, the frozen feature extractor will be tested on 4 environments completely missing from the VAE training data:

* `AlienNoFrameskip-v4`
* `AsteroidsNoFrameskip-v4`
* `AtlantisNoFrameskip-v4`
* `IceHockeyNoFrameskip-v4`

